        ":question/id": "d67d847a-8cc9-599e-9782-16c3ef20921d",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-77065",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/b6/b614e2c3e6abf0d7ddb8399aa7ea78b7a0e618a965ee7b5481edf08310a45031.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=1$ to $t=2?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,\\,6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}-3)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}+9+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{t}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+3t){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(8+6)-(1+3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
