        ":question/problem": "![](images/9b/9b83db0bb11b8e4bbcc4b04b7bf696879d4d7f4732d6aef7d005f9da2b625526.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨2{t}^{3/2},\\,3t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=3.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,3⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{t})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9t+9} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3(t+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
