        ":question/problem": "![](images/54/54cd1a0532bbdbe76bd81d5ad737f6d3e9f5293d08ea78758865b3b71850e346.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},\\,2t⟩$ from $t=1$ to $t=e.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t}-t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t}-t)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-2+{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+2+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t}+t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{t}+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
