        ":question/problem": "![](images/e6/e6de928031610e2aaf55e3dd753449f766e7dc028a6618087e7489224a73ea7d.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
